WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.540
A litre of plasma carries about three millilitres of oxygen, but a litre of your

2
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:09.940
blood carries about two hundred.

3
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.320
What makes up the difference, and how does blood give it up in the right places?

4
00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:20.743
Plasma is mostly water, and oxygen dissolves poorly in water.

5
00:00:22.043 --> 00:00:27.223
The difference rides on hemoglobin, an iron-containing protein packed inside red

6
00:00:27.223 --> 00:00:28.223
blood cells.

7
00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:34.227
Saturation is the share of hemoglobin's binding sites that hold oxygen.

8
00:00:35.547 --> 00:00:39.867
Plot it against oxygen pressure and mark where blood loads and where it unloads.

9
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.420
Blood loads in the lungs, where oxygen pressure is high.

10
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.640
At rest, it leaves the tissues at a much lower pressure.

11
00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:55.443
Each decilitre of blood has a maximum load of oxygen on hemoglobin, its carrying

12
00:00:55.443 --> 00:01:00.703
capacity. The load actually carried is capacity times saturation.

13
00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:07.483
Hemoglobin releases that capacity times the drop in saturation, from lung to tissue.

14
00:01:08.743 --> 00:01:11.683
That drop is the gap between two points on one curve.

15
00:01:12.683 --> 00:01:16.783
Try a simple carrier first, with one binding site per molecule.

16
00:01:18.143 --> 00:01:21.563
Give it a loose grip, half full at about the same pressure as blood.

17
00:01:22.823 --> 00:01:25.583
In the lungs, it fills only to about four-fifths.

18
00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:29.367
So tighten its grip, until it fills in the lungs.

19
00:01:30.587 --> 00:01:32.827
What happens when that blood reaches the tissue?

20
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:45.300
It holds on. At tissue pressure it is still nearly full, so it releases almost

21
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:51.160
nothing. Between lung and tissue pressures, a one-site carrier faces a trade-off:

22
00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:53.280
fill well, or give up a lot.

23
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:56.333
Hemoglobin escapes the trade-off.

24
00:01:57.593 --> 00:01:58.693
Its curve is S-shaped.

25
00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:04.413
Near the top it is nearly flat, so a modest fall in lung pressure costs little.

26
00:02:05.973 --> 00:02:07.253
Below that, it falls steeply.

27
00:02:08.233 --> 00:02:12.353
At rest, the tissue point sits at the shoulder, where the steep part begins.

28
00:02:13.693 --> 00:02:16.053
Blood gives up about a quarter of its oxygen.

29
00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:20.687
The other three-quarters comes back unused, as a reserve.

30
00:02:21.947 --> 00:02:27.107
In hard work, the oxygen pressure of blood leaving active tissue can fall into the

31
00:02:27.107 --> 00:02:31.107
steep middle. Now the same blood gives up about two-thirds.

32
00:02:32.267 --> 00:02:36.467
Why the S? Hemoglobin has four binding sites.

33
00:02:37.747 --> 00:02:40.987
In a simple picture, the molecule switches shape as a whole.

34
00:02:42.287 --> 00:02:46.327
Each oxygen that binds makes the switch to the high-affinity shape more likely.

35
00:02:47.517 --> 00:02:52.437
So once some sites hold oxygen, the rest fill with a smaller rise in pressure.

36
00:02:53.817 --> 00:02:55.697
Emptying runs the same way in reverse.

37
00:02:56.957 --> 00:02:58.437
That is cooperative binding.

38
00:02:59.517 --> 00:03:04.837
Between these lung and tissue pressures, a one-site carrier cannot both load fully

39
00:03:04.837 --> 00:03:11.357
and unload a lot. Cooperative binding lets one carrier load fully in the lungs, and

40
00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:12.977
still unload a lot in the tissues.
